<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Diaspora.Modules.Integrations</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Diaspora.Modules.Integrations</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      The Integration Foundation (Phase B22). The external-boundary infrastructure the frontend's
      Phase 16 register found no contract behind: a connection registry, an OAuth authorization-code
      exchange with server-side token custody, a proxied calendar boundary, synchronisation with a
      stable source-to-external mapping, an inbound webhook intake, and an outbound HTTP boundary
      with an allow-list.

      What this module owns: the fact that a member has connected an external account, what that
      grant permits, when it expires, when it last worked and when it last failed; the single-use
      authorization request that makes an OAuth callback verifiable; the encrypted custody of a
      provider's tokens; the mapping between a Diaspora entity and the external record written for
      it; and the record of every provider callback received.

      What it deliberately does not own, and never duplicates:

        Money is B15's. This module creates no payment, no ledger entry and no wallet movement, and
        an architecture test asserts the assembly names no financial type. The gateway boundary
        B15 already owns (IPaymentGateway, IPaymentWebhookProcessor, ProcessedWebhookEvent) is left
        exactly as it is.

        Delivery is B13's. This module publishes integration events and contributes a category,
        five templates and five rules through B13's own contributor seams. It composes no
        notification, sends no push and owns no device.

        Geography is B21's. IRoutingProvider and IGeocodingProvider stay where they are; this
        module provides the outbound HTTP boundary an adapter for either would call through.

        Identity is Identity's. There is no second user, no second session and no second credential
        store for the platform's own authentication. An external identity is a connection in this
        registry; whether somebody may sign in is decided where it always was.

        Resilience, scheduling, secrets, caching, rate limiting and dead-lettering are B17's.
        Nothing here implements a retry loop, a breaker, a scheduler, a lock or a secret store —
        every one is resolved from the platform.

      Registers entirely through B2.6 contributor interfaces: no edit to the Platform Kernel, to
      Identity, or to Program.cs.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />

    <!--
      Data Protection and IHttpClientFactory both come from the ASP.NET Core shared framework,
      which this project already has through Diaspora.Platform's FrameworkReference. A package
      reference for either would pin a version beside the one the host actually loads.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    One module reference, and it is the same shape Conversations already uses.

    Notifications, for its catalogue contributor seams only — INotificationCategoryContributor,
    INotificationTemplateContributor, INotificationRuleContributor. FRONTEND-PHASE16-GAP-017 asks
    for an `integrations` category and five templates, and B13.3 recorded that they wait on "grant
    and sync lifecycle events". This module publishes those events, so it contributes the wording
    that renders them rather than asking B13 to hold knowledge of a module composed 800 later.

    Notably absent: Financial. A payment provider's callback is B15's and stays B15's.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.SharedKernel\Diaspora.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Contracts\Diaspora.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Platform\Diaspora.Platform.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Application\Diaspora.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Persistence\Diaspora.Persistence.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Modules.Notifications\Diaspora.Modules.Notifications.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
